The Lamplit Inn. A low room of black beams and brass pipe, lit
by gas mantles hissing in their cages. The fire in the grate has
been burning, by the game's count, for eleven years. Rain and
fog press at the shutters.
  Old Bessa the innkeeper is here, wiping the same glass.
  Dana the warden is here, oiling a sabre she never draws.
  Elias Thorne, a lighthouse keeper, is here, asleep at a
  table. (idle 8y)

[narrator · hollowmere]
Somewhere under the floor, the bell rings once.
Dana looks up.
Dana says, "Did somebody fix it again?"
Nobody answers. Dana looks at the chalkboard over the bar, where
the score from the drill is still chalked up: Dana 31, Tinker 17,
Mori 15, Pip "not counting".
Dana says, "Pip."
Dana says, "I don't know why, but I feel like I owe Pip a gin."
Elias Thorne wakes. He lifts his head off the table and looks at
the chalkboard for a long moment.
Elias Thorne says, "Good."
Elias Thorne leaves the game.
Dana says, "Thorne?"
Bessa says, "Rain's set in for the night, love."

